From 4332131c80b51011e7d2e7add55b6671a494ef24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Turbo-LAG-GUY <jordandwj@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 02:10:35 -0500
Subject: [RFC PATCH] drm/etnaviv: keep the core in reset across runtime
 power-down

On the Amlogic A311D, the NPU (VIPNano-QI, GC8000 rev 0x7120) has its
own power domain, NNA, which meson-ee-pwrc switches off when the NPU is
runtime suspended and back on at resume. The domain has no reset of its
own, and etnaviv releases the core's reset only once, in
etnaviv_gpu_init(). Runtime resume just re-runs etnaviv_gpu_hw_init(),
so after every power cycle the core starts from whatever state its logic
came up in.

On a Libre Computer AML-A311D-CC ("Alta") running Mesa's Teflon
delegate, this now and then hard-locks the SoC at the first job after a
power-up. Nothing reaches the kernel log; only the hardware watchdog
brings the board back. It happened in 3 of 15 program starts, each of
which began with a power-up, and never in about 2,000 jobs submitted
while the NPU was already powered.

Amlogic's own driver never switches the NPU domain off in normal
operation. When its reset path does cycle the power, it keeps the reset
asserted throughout, waits 10 ms after power-up before releasing it,
and waits 20 ms after.

Do the same across runtime PM. Assert the reset in runtime suspend once
the clocks are off, so that the domain goes down and comes back up with
the core held in reset. On resume, release it with the waits from
Amlogic's driver, and soft-reset the core as etnaviv_gpu_init() does
before running etnaviv_gpu_hw_init().

With this change, 70 runtime resumes on the A311D (50 in one process,
each after at least 10 s powered down, and 20 separate program starts)
gave correct results without a lockup. The waits add about 30 ms to
every runtime resume, and to driver init, on cores that have a reset
line. They come from Amlogic's driver and are not tuned.

The fix uses the reset line handling added by the commit below, which
releases the reset at init but never asserts it again.

The analysis, this patch and its description were written with an AI
coding assistant (Claude Opus 5.5 in Claude Code); see the notes below
the --- line. I reproduced the problem and tested the fix on my board,
and I have reviewed the patch.

Fixes: 6bde14ba5f7e ("drm/etnaviv: add optional reset support")
---
RFC, for these reasons:

- Only built and run as a backport, on the Libre Computer 6.12.73
  kernel (6.12.y-lc) with 6bde14ba5f7e, c182a2ae1388, 1006d1f7c03d and
  4720915ab482 backported, plus a local patch that disables SH_EU clock
  gating on 0x7120. This patch applies to v7.4-rc1 (base below), where
  etnaviv_gpu.c is unchanged since v7.3, but it has not been built or
  run there. The tested build also had a local module parameter that
  keeps the core powered after init. For the 70 resumes above it was
  overridden at runtime by writing "auto" to power/control; apart from
  that parameter, this patch is that build.
- The 10 ms / 20 ms waits are Amlogic's values. Are shorter waits known
  to be enough, or should the long ones be limited to the SoCs that
  need them?
- Should this live in meson-ee-pwrc instead, the way it handles the VPU
  domain's resets? etnaviv already owns the reset line, so a reset in
  the domain driver would have to share it.
- Only the combination was tested: the reset held across the power
  cycle, the waits, and the soft reset on resume. I don't know which
  part is needed.

Not done: the lockup itself was never captured (no serial console, and
nothing reached the journal), so the failing state is a guess. One
candidate: this core runs with sec_mode == ETNA_SEC_KERNEL, and
etnaviv_iommuv2_restore_sec() skips all MMU setup when
VIVS_MMUv2_SEC_CONTROL already reads back as enabled, which after a
power-up without a reset comes down to chance.

Keeping the NPU powered, as Amlogic's driver does, also avoids the
lockups. With a local pm_runtime_forbid() right after
etnaviv_gpu_init(), 53 program starts and a 2,000-job soak ran without
one. Writing "on" to power/control after boot works too, but the write
itself still goes through one power-up without a reset.

Amlogic's driver here is galcore from
https://github.com/khadas/android_vendor_amlogic_npu (branch
khadas-vim3-pie), hal/os/linux/kernel/platform/amlogic/
gc_hal_kernel_platform_amlogic.c, _Reset(). Its platform ops have no
setPower, so the NPU domain stays on after probe.

Test setup: Mesa 26.1.0-devel (Teflon), ai_edge_litert, the quantized
MobileNet v1 from the Teflon docs, with every result checked against a
CPU run. hw_job_limit=1 was set; it was an earlier attempt to avoid the
lockups and didn't help.

AI assistance (Documentation/process/generated-content.rst): Claude Opus
5.5 (Anthropic), in Claude Code on the board, with access to the kernel
sources and logs. My prompts were, in short, "find out why the NPU
freezes the board, check upstream for fixes, and fix it", plus approval
of each test, build and install step. It wrote the analysis, the patch
and this text, and ran the tests through a test kit; I ran all root
steps.

 drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c | 50 +++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c
index 5c23182..47c1e65 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/etnaviv/etnaviv_gpu.c
@@ -179,21 +179,25 @@ static int etnaviv_gpu_reset_deassert(struct etnaviv_gpu *gpu)
 {
 	int ret;
 
+	if (!gpu->rst)
+		return 0;
+
 	/*
-	 * 32 core clock cycles (slowest clock) required before deassertion
-	 * 1 microsecond might match all implementations without computation
+	 * 32 core clock cycles (slowest clock) required before deassertion.
+	 * Wait 10 ms, as the Amlogic vendor driver does after powering up the
+	 * A311D NPU, which also gives the power domain time to settle.
 	 */
-	usleep_range(1, 2);
+	usleep_range(10000, 11000);
 
 	ret = reset_control_deassert(gpu->rst);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
 	/*
-	 * 128 core clock cycles (slowest clock) required before any activity on AHB
-	 * 1 microsecond might match all implementations without computation
+	 * 128 core clock cycles (slowest clock) required before any activity on AHB.
+	 * Wait 20 ms, as the Amlogic vendor driver does.
 	 */
-	usleep_range(1, 2);
+	msleep(20);
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -1750,11 +1754,20 @@ static int etnaviv_gpu_hw_resume(struct etnaviv_gpu *gpu)
 		return ret;
 
 	etnaviv_gpu_update_clock(gpu);
-	etnaviv_gpu_hw_init(gpu);
+
+	/*
+	 * The core may have been powered off while suspended. Reset it as
+	 * etnaviv_gpu_init() does before programming it, so that no state from
+	 * the power cycle is left behind: an MMU that reads back as enabled,
+	 * for example, makes the MMU restore skip programming the page tables.
+	 */
+	ret = etnaviv_hw_reset(gpu);
+	if (!ret)
+		etnaviv_gpu_hw_init(gpu);
 
 	mutex_unlock(&gpu->lock);
 
-	return 0;
+	return ret;
 }
 
 static int
@@ -2002,6 +2015,7 @@ static int etnaviv_gpu_rpm_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct etnaviv_gpu *gpu = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	u32 idle, mask;
+	int ret;
 
 	/* If there are any jobs in the HW queue, we're not idle */
 	if (atomic_read(&gpu->sched.credit_count))
@@ -2021,7 +2035,20 @@ static int etnaviv_gpu_rpm_suspend(struct device *dev)
 
 	gpu->state = ETNA_GPU_STATE_IDENTIFIED;
 
-	return etnaviv_gpu_clk_disable(gpu);
+	ret = etnaviv_gpu_clk_disable(gpu);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Hold the core in reset while it is suspended, so that it comes back
+	 * up in reset if its power domain is switched off in the meantime.
+	 * Some domains, like the NNA domain of the Amlogic G12B, have no reset
+	 * of their own. Runtime resume releases the reset again.
+	 */
+	if (reset_control_assert(gpu->rst))
+		dev_warn(dev, "failed to assert reset\n");
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int etnaviv_gpu_rpm_resume(struct device *dev)
@@ -2035,9 +2062,12 @@ static int etnaviv_gpu_rpm_resume(struct device *dev)
 
 	/* Re-initialise the basic hardware state */
 	if (gpu->state == ETNA_GPU_STATE_IDENTIFIED) {
-		ret = etnaviv_gpu_hw_resume(gpu);
+		ret = etnaviv_gpu_reset_deassert(gpu);
+		if (!ret)
+			ret = etnaviv_gpu_hw_resume(gpu);
 		if (ret) {
 			etnaviv_gpu_clk_disable(gpu);
+			reset_control_assert(gpu->rst);
 			return ret;
 		}
 	}

